  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "A bi-objective routing framework couples high-resolution AERMOD PM2.5 dispersion modeling with a gradient-based shortest-path algorithm to map Pareto-optimal trade-offs between travel time and pollution exposure. Applied across 790,000 origin-destination pairs and 1.48 million daily commuters in New York City, exposure-aware routes reduced cumulative PM2.5 exposure by roughly 38% while incurring a median travel time penalty of about 28%. The algorithm executes in 30 milliseconds per query and identifies an average of 4.5 frontier paths compared to 2.4 using traditional uniform weight sampling.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that micro-level pollution variations between adjacent streets can be leveraged for health-optimal navigation without causing severe computational overhead or excessive travel delays at metropolitan scales."
    }
